rows=[(498, 'the-red-sari-in-the-trunk', 'The red sari in the trunk', 'The mother’s trunk holds childhood and the grief carried while raising her children. The fold of red Banarasi sari is the quiet hinge of the poem; suggest memory through cloth in the trunk without staging every toy or inventing the mother.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 tender oil-pastel and gouache interior still life, slightly elevated oblique view into an ordinary open metal household storage trunk resting on a plain floor. The lid is supported at a plausible backward angle by its hinges; dark blue-grey painted metal, simple clasp with no lock dangling. A folded deep red silk sari fills only part of the trunk, a restrained gold woven border partly hidden by a loosely folded pale cotton cloth. The red fabric shows soft compressed folds and a subtle lustre where ordinary side daylight reaches it; shadowed interior stays unresolved. Crop part of the far lid, avoid making a catalogue display. No visible toys, dolls, guns, jewel boxes, sentimental photographs, hands, people, letters or readable text. No rust spectacle, brocade microdetail or bridal luxury campaign. Material memory, calm and emotionally unresolved.'), (501, 'where-the-road-enters-mist', 'Where the road enters mist', 'The poem experiences Jatinga through a wet winding road, trees and slow fog. An imagined bend holds the feeling of entering that quiet without pretending to reconstruct or photograph a specific surveyed location.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 restrained watercolor and loose dark ink landscape: an ordinary narrow paved hill road curves gently from lower left around a wooded slope, its far bend disappearing gradually into real low cloud. Evergreen trees of uneven height partly hide the bend; a dark earth bank supports the road on one side, modest wet roadside vegetation on the other. Overcast soft light, wet grey road only mildly reflective, mist occludes distant trees and softens them progressively. Road width and perspective coherent, no impossible switchback, spectacular cliff or magical white fog curls. No cars, people, traffic signs, village, named landmarks, mountains lined symmetrically or sun shafts. An imagined response to the Jatinga poem, not a documented view. Broken paper edges, varied leaf masses, detailed nearest road verge only.'), (505, 'the-leaf-about-to-let-go', 'The leaf about to let go', 'A mother addresses her growing son during autumn, when trees seem to write farewell letters to leaves. One remaining leaf on a branch carries loving release without depicting an invented child or turning tears into spectacle.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 spare coloured-pencil drawing with a thin ochre watercolor wash. Close view of a plain deciduous twig extending from the left edge into open pale autumn air, one small ochre leaf still attached near its end by a visible continuous petiole, another empty short leaf stem nearby. Leaf is slightly curled, uneven edges with a few natural notches, not perfectly symmetrical or a heart symbol. Twig tapers naturally and joins its smaller offshoots. Right two thirds remain almost empty ivory with a soft hint of cool distant light; no tree panorama, falling leaf shower, words, letter paper, people, mother, child, eyes or tears. Gentle available daylight, selective pencil work on the attachment and one vein, edges allowed to fade. Tenderness of holding and releasing, not a motivational stock image.')]
items=[]
